1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,880
This is KubeSage, an AI SRE team for Kubernetes.

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:06,636
It runs on a real two-machine cluster:

3
00:00:06,636 --> 00:00:08,303
a desktop and a laptop.

4
00:00:08,303 --> 00:00:14,830
This video replays runs recorded on that cluster, step by step, without changing anything.

5
00:00:16,134 --> 00:00:17,992
KubeSage tells two stories.

6
00:00:17,992 --> 00:00:20,194
First, the cluster wastes money.

7
00:00:20,194 --> 00:00:22,809
KubeSage finds the waste and fixes it.

8
00:00:22,809 --> 00:00:24,530
Second, something breaks.

9
00:00:24,530 --> 00:00:29,416
A team of AI agents finds the cause and writes the fix as a git commit.

10
00:00:29,416 --> 00:00:34,784
The agent chat on the left shows how the agents hand their work to each other.

11
00:00:36,081 --> 00:00:40,695
Each slab is a real machine, standing in for a priced cloud VM.

12
00:00:40,695 --> 00:00:44,798
Each glass box is a pod, as tall as the CPU it reserves.

13
00:00:44,798 --> 00:00:48,607
The glowing core inside is the CPU it actually uses.

14
00:00:48,607 --> 00:00:53,661
Tall glass around a small core means paying for capacity nobody uses.

15
00:00:53,661 --> 00:00:57,031
Right now, every demo pod sits on the desktop.

16
00:00:58,279 --> 00:01:00,733
Analyze reads thirty days of history:

17
00:01:00,733 --> 00:01:04,579
a synthetic backfill, plus live samples from this cluster.

18
00:01:04,579 --> 00:01:06,171
Statistics cost nothing.

19
00:01:06,171 --> 00:01:12,405
One low-effort model call triages every workload, and only the harder ones get more reasoning.

20
00:01:12,405 --> 00:01:20,429
This analysis found thirty-three dollars a month in node-pool savings, at list prices, for about one cent of model calls.

21
00:01:21,652 --> 00:01:23,382
This is the router at work.

22
00:01:23,382 --> 00:01:25,687
Healthy workloads stop after triage.

23
00:01:25,687 --> 00:01:31,580
Routine ones get medium reasoning, and complex ones, like a memory leak, get high reasoning.

24
00:01:31,580 --> 00:01:38,882
Guardrails then hold every request above the usage that was measured, and keep the scaling settings within bounds.

25
00:01:40,107 --> 00:01:42,376
Apply sends the plan to the cluster.

26
00:01:42,376 --> 00:01:45,905
Requests are right-sized, and KEDA autoscaling is added.

27
00:01:45,905 --> 00:01:53,782
The pods move to the laptop, which stands in for a cheaper machine type, as a real Apply did on the twenty-eighth of October.

28
00:01:53,782 --> 00:01:58,257
Meanwhile, report-worker scales to zero until its nightly batch window.

29
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:00,672
Now, real traffic:

30
00:02:00,672 --> 00:02:03,322
sixty requests a second at checkout-web.

31
00:02:03,322 --> 00:02:09,682
These numbers come from a run recorded on the twenty-seventh of October, shown six times faster.

32
00:02:09,682 --> 00:02:15,577
CPU climbs past the seventy percent target, and KEDA scales checkout-web out to six pods.

33
00:02:15,577 --> 00:02:21,009
While new pods started, the sampled intervals showed twenty-three failed requests.

34
00:02:21,009 --> 00:02:21,738
After that:

35
00:02:21,738 --> 00:02:27,700
zero errors, and a p ninety-five of one hundred seventy to two hundred forty milliseconds.

36
00:02:35,595 --> 00:02:36,123
Act two:

37
00:02:36,123 --> 00:02:37,246
something breaks.

38
00:02:37,246 --> 00:02:40,876
The memory test case starts ledger-cache on the laptop.

39
00:02:40,876 --> 00:02:48,732
It tries to hold seven hundred megabytes under a five hundred and twelve megabyte limit, so it is killed, and restarts.

40
00:02:48,732 --> 00:02:51,373
The alert rule fires ContainerOOMKilled.

41
00:02:51,373 --> 00:02:54,673
With auto-run on, the agent team starts by itself.

42
00:02:54,673 --> 00:02:59,492
On the live cluster it first waits forty-five seconds for related alerts.

43
00:02:59,492 --> 00:03:03,585
The run that follows is the real one, recorded for this alert.

44
00:03:04,814 --> 00:03:08,785
Four sub-agents on a small model gather evidence in parallel:

45
00:03:08,785 --> 00:03:12,951
metrics, the Kubernetes API, Alertmanager, and security posture.

46
00:03:12,951 --> 00:03:17,247
Every finding must cite a signal they collected, or it is dropped.

47
00:03:17,247 --> 00:03:22,130
Then the Thinker, a large model, reads the findings and the platform rules.

48
00:03:22,130 --> 00:03:25,255
It finds the root cause, and plans four actions.

49
00:03:25,255 --> 00:03:31,114
The chat on the left rebuilds this run's reports and plan as hand-offs between the agents.

50
00:03:32,405 --> 00:03:36,234
The Executor writes the fix as a git commit on its own branch:

51
00:03:36,234 --> 00:03:44,324
a bigger memory limit, two security fixes, and a proposal for a memory-optimised node pool, which needs a separate Terraform apply.

52
00:03:44,324 --> 00:03:49,512
Its edits are bound to their actions, and the Kubernetes changes pass policy checks.

53
00:03:49,512 --> 00:03:53,959
Sixteen seconds and two cents, from the start of the run to this commit.

54
00:03:53,959 --> 00:03:59,765
A person reads the diff, dry-runs it, and decides whether to apply it, or open a pull request.

55
00:04:01,054 --> 00:04:04,462
This incident cost about two cents in model calls.

56
00:04:04,462 --> 00:04:11,073
Every finding must cite collected evidence, and agent fixes become commits that a person reviews.

57
00:04:11,073 --> 00:04:13,936
A person decides what reaches the cluster.

58
00:04:13,936 --> 00:04:15,094
That is KubeSage.
